Subject-perspective review saved to `docs/content-analysis/perspective-reviews/Freddie-Mercury/2026-08-28_020004/subject.md` (SHA verified against context.json before review).

**Verdict:** revise — trust strained, value high, delight clear_hit. **2 blockers, 5 concerns.**

**Strongest hit:** the Freestone "Freddie loved laughing" paragraph and its close — "The shyness was real and the joy was real. What he rationed was access." It hosts the subject's own advocate's correction of the sad-clown myth without resolving the ambivalence in the thesis's favor, and it must survive every later pass.

**Blockers:**
1. **SUBJECT-B1** — The Mary Austin FAQ claims "she was holding his hand when he died." No checked account supports it (accounts place Dave Clark at the bedside, Jim Hutton present per his memoir, Freestone in the house, Mary phoned *after*); my own search this run confirmed the packet's sweep. It's a fabricated intimate detail on a SERP-surfaced answer, and it displaces Hutton — the exact heteronormative rewrite of Mercury's story the draft otherwise avoids.
2. **SUBJECT-B2** — The Munich section turns his mild on-tape line ("I buy a lot of shitty things... I get them back a few years later," followed by "No, not really" when asked if he was disillusioned) into gifts "pawned by people who'd wanted the wallet" — invented mechanism and motive that the stress-arrow argument then leans on.

**Highest-priority concern (SUBJECT-C1):** the draft systematically clips Mercury's de-dramatizing codas — Dolezal's pretence confession ended "It's fun.", and the scars quote ended with him insisting "everybody has that kind of thing" — darkening his register toward pure confession three-for-three.

**One finding in the draft's favor, flagged for synthesis:** the packet's CLM-08 misattribution charge is half wrong. "Every day is Fasching for me" *is* in the on-file Wigg transcript, auto-captioned as "flashing" right after his Fasching riff — the draft's "he told Wigg" is supported and shouldn't be cut.

Sources consulted beyond the packet: the on-file 1985 Wigg transcript, plus [Smooth Radio](https://www.smoothradio.com/artists/queen/jim-hutton-freddie-mercury-death-interview/), [IrishCentral](https://www.irishcentral.com/culture/entertainment/freddie-mercurys-irish-boyfriend-jim-hutton), and [All That's Interesting](https://allthatsinteresting.com/freddie-mercury-death) for the deathbed check.
